WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.149
In 1872, the French writer Jules Verne published a famous novel:

00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:14.723
Around the World in Eighty Days.

00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:20.038
The hero is Phileas Fogg, a gentleman in London who lives by the clock.

00:00:20.318 --> 00:00:25.203
Fogg bets half his money that he can travel around the whole world in eighty days.

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:30.651
Tonight — Wednesday, the second of October, 1872 — at a quarter to nine,

00:00:30.651 --> 00:00:32.374
his train leaves London.

00:00:32.654 --> 00:00:35.001
We follow his route, starting today.

00:00:35.281 --> 00:00:37.756
Look at the map: only London is lit.

00:00:38.036 --> 00:00:40.084
Day by day, we light the rest.

00:00:40.364 --> 00:00:44.161
One more name: Passepartout, Fogg's new French valet.

00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:46.340
His watch is four minutes slow.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.857
Remember the watch.

00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:50.868
First stop: Fogg's street, Savile Row.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.008
This is Savile Row, a quiet street in Mayfair.

00:01:08.288 --> 00:01:09.739
The novel starts here.

00:01:10.019 --> 00:01:14.243
Phileas Fogg lives on this street — at No. 7, the book says.

00:01:14.523 --> 00:01:17.894
Tonight he will leave from that door to go round the world.

00:01:18.174 --> 00:01:23.166
Look in the shop windows: rolls of cloth, chalk marks, half-made jackets.

00:01:23.446 --> 00:01:27.307
In 1872 this was already a street of tailors.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.675
It still is.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:33.734
The tailor Henry Poole opened his door on the Row in 1846.

00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:38.025
The shop is still here — at No. 15 since 1982.

00:01:38.305 --> 00:01:44.349
The firm says that in 1865 they made a short evening jacket for the Prince of Wales

00:01:44.349 --> 00:01:47.990
— the grandfather, they say, of the dinner jacket.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:52.770
One more stop: on the roof of No. 3, in January 1969,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.846
the Beatles played their famous rooftop concert.

00:01:57.126 --> 00:01:59.750
And one small secret about No. 7.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.105
Verne wrote that the playwright Sheridan died there in 1814.

00:02:04.385 --> 00:02:08.993
In fact Sheridan lived at No. 14, and died in 1816.

00:02:09.273 --> 00:02:11.684
No. 14 has a plaque for him.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:14.951
Fogg has no plaque — he lived only in the book.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.027
Fogg's day, hour by hour.

00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:32.758
He gets up at eight.

00:02:33.038 --> 00:02:35.747
Tea and toast at twenty-three minutes past eight.

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:40.375
Shaving water at nine thirty-seven, at exactly thirty degrees

00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:42.086
— eighty-six Fahrenheit.

00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:45.459
He leaves at half past eleven; home at midnight.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:48.875
This morning, at twenty-nine minutes past eleven,

00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:51.179
Passepartout walked into this house.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:53.294
A Parisian, about thirty.

00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:58.907
He was a singer, a circus rider, a gymnastics teacher, a fireman in Paris.

00:02:59.187 --> 00:03:01.726
Then ten English houses in five years.

00:03:02.006 --> 00:03:03.755
He wants a quiet life now.

00:03:04.035 --> 00:03:07.000
We like him already — the man with the slow watch.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.429
Half past eleven.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:23.312
Fogg leaves the house and walks to his club.

00:03:23.592 --> 00:03:28.477
He counts his steps as he goes: one thousand one hundred and fifty-one of them.

00:03:28.757 --> 00:03:33.060
His way runs down Savile Row, right into Burlington Gardens,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.288
then left down Old Bond Street to Piccadilly.

00:03:36.568 --> 00:03:40.259
Across the road, and down the long slope of St James's Street.

00:03:40.539 --> 00:03:43.526
At the bottom he turns east, into Pall Mall.

00:03:43.806 --> 00:03:48.912
From his front door to the club door is one thousand one hundred and twenty metres:

00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:50.142
about one kilometre.

00:03:50.422 --> 00:03:53.515
So each step is about ninety-seven centimetres.

00:03:53.795 --> 00:03:54.990
He never hurries.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.894
And this is the street he is walking into.

00:03:58.174 --> 00:04:03.102
Pall Mall had gas lamps in 1807, one of the first streets in London.

00:04:03.382 --> 00:04:07.755
Sixty-five years before Fogg, the lamps here were already the future.

00:04:08.035 --> 00:04:11.768
This is clubland: a row of private clubs on the south side.

00:04:12.048 --> 00:04:13.755
Three of them stand together.

00:04:14.035 --> 00:04:20.317
In front of us, the grand stone front with the tall windows: 104 Pall Mall,

00:04:20.317 --> 00:04:24.254
the Reform Club, by Charles Barry, opened 1841.

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:25.963
That one is Fogg's.

00:04:26.243 --> 00:04:33.216
A little to the left, No. 106, the Travellers, Barry's first Italian palazzo,

00:04:33.216 --> 00:04:34.755
finished in 1832.

00:04:35.035 --> 00:04:38.726
And beyond it, No. 107, the Athenaeum.

00:04:39.006 --> 00:04:41.310
One turn of the head covers all three.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.157
This is the door of Fogg's club: the Reform Club.

00:04:59.437 --> 00:05:02.978
It is real, and still here — 104 Pall Mall.

00:05:03.258 --> 00:05:09.167
Its founders had backed the Reform Act of 1832 — that is where the name comes from.

00:05:09.447 --> 00:05:15.271
In the book, Fogg spends every day here until midnight: meals, newspapers, cards.

00:05:15.551 --> 00:05:19.732
The architect Charles Barry finished the building in 1841.

00:05:20.012 --> 00:05:22.508
Stone outside; inside, this hall.

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:24.516
They were down in the basement.

00:05:24.796 --> 00:05:30.001
Here Fogg ate his breakfast: grilled fish with Reading sauce — remember that sauce.

00:05:30.281 --> 00:05:32.350
Then Barry's design for the front.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:37.217
Somewhere in there, Fogg cuts his newspaper's pages and reads The Times.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.584
Verne describes the inside of the club with much detail.

00:06:01.864 --> 00:06:03.123
Mosaic floors.

00:06:03.403 --> 00:06:07.499
A gallery under a dome, held up by twenty red stone columns.

00:06:07.779 --> 00:06:09.294
Blue painted windows.

00:06:09.574 --> 00:06:12.667
A dining room with nine windows onto a garden.

00:06:12.947 --> 00:06:15.144
Compare that with Barry's real building.

00:06:15.424 --> 00:06:17.408
Which of these details is real?

00:06:17.688 --> 00:06:23.370
A dining-room with nine windows onto the garden. Exact. Nine tall casement windows

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.736
in the coffee-room's south wall, and the garden is still there.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.515
Evening, at the club.

00:06:49.795 --> 00:06:54.384
Fogg plays whist with five fellow members — four hands at the table,

00:06:54.384 --> 00:06:56.408
the same five men every night.

00:06:56.688 --> 00:07:02.182
In the story — only in the story — a thief has stolen fifty-five thousand pounds

00:07:02.182 --> 00:07:03.899
from the Bank of England.

00:07:04.179 --> 00:07:06.120
Where could such a man hide now?

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.894
So a newspaper, the Daily Telegraph, counts the days:

00:07:09.894 --> 00:07:12.992
you can now go around the world in eighty days.

00:07:13.272 --> 00:07:15.576
At the table this becomes a wager.

00:07:15.856 --> 00:07:20.763
Andrew Stuart, an engineer, bets four thousand pounds that it cannot be done.

00:07:21.043 --> 00:07:24.968
Fogg bets twenty thousand pounds — half his money — that it can.

00:07:25.248 --> 00:07:27.403
Look at the card: six names.

00:07:27.683 --> 00:07:29.560
Stuart first, Fogg last.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.693
Fogg must be back in this same room on Saturday, the twenty-first of December,

00:07:34.693 --> 00:07:36.560
at a quarter to nine at night.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.019
Fogg's motto: 'The unforeseen does not exist.'

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.048
How could anyone believe this plan?

00:08:05.328 --> 00:08:07.717
Because the world had just become smaller.

00:08:07.997 --> 00:08:13.300
When one of the card players said the world was big, Fogg answered with three words:

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.184
'It was once.'

00:08:14.464 --> 00:08:15.957
Look at the three lights.

00:08:16.237 --> 00:08:19.970
The Suez Canal opened in November 1869.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:25.583
The American railroad, coast to coast, was joined in May 1869.

00:08:25.863 --> 00:08:30.684
The last rail link across India closed the gap in March 1870.

00:08:30.964 --> 00:08:35.273
Three doors, all opened within ten months, two years before tonight.

00:08:35.553 --> 00:08:37.174
Now watch the line draw.

00:08:37.454 --> 00:08:39.886
London to Suez: seven days.

00:08:40.166 --> 00:08:43.046
Suez to Bombay, by ship: thirteen.

00:08:43.326 --> 00:08:45.929
Bombay to Calcutta, by rail: three.

00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:48.684
Calcutta to Hong Kong: thirteen.

00:08:48.964 --> 00:08:51.439
Hong Kong to Yokohama: six.

00:08:51.719 --> 00:08:54.834
Yokohama to San Francisco: twenty-two.

00:08:55.114 --> 00:08:58.271
San Francisco to New York, by rail: seven.

00:08:58.551 --> 00:09:00.983
New York back to London: nine.

00:09:01.263 --> 00:09:02.948
Add them up: eighty.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.664
Twenty-five past seven.

00:09:36.944 --> 00:09:38.757
Fogg comes home from the club.

00:09:39.037 --> 00:09:43.965
He calls Passepartout and says: 'We start for Dover and Calais in ten minutes.'

00:09:44.245 --> 00:09:48.896
Dover is on the English coast; Calais is across the sea, in France.

00:09:49.176 --> 00:09:50.157
Where to?

00:09:50.437 --> 00:09:52.122
'We are going round the world.'

00:09:52.402 --> 00:09:53.639
'In eighty days.'

00:09:53.919 --> 00:09:56.906
Passepartout, remember, wanted a quiet life.

00:09:57.186 --> 00:09:58.530
Now help him pack.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:02.629
One carpet-bag — one travel bag — must hold everything.

00:10:02.909 --> 00:10:05.704
Two shirts and three pairs of stockings each.

00:10:05.984 --> 00:10:08.395
A mackintosh and a travelling-cloak.

00:10:08.675 --> 00:10:13.496
The red-bound Bradshaw — a thick book of train times, hundreds of pages.

00:10:13.776 --> 00:10:17.808
And a fat roll of Bank of England notes: twenty thousand pounds.

00:10:18.088 --> 00:10:20.029
No books — the house has none.

00:10:20.309 --> 00:10:22.698
The electric clock stays on the mantel.

00:10:22.978 --> 00:10:25.837
And so, tonight, does the gas — remember that.

00:10:26.117 --> 00:10:29.680
Two shirts, three pairs of stockings, and half a fortune.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.490
Fogg and Passepartout take a cab at the end of Savile Row — probably a hansom,

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.531
a fast two-wheeled horse cab.

00:11:10.811 --> 00:11:14.907
To the driver, Fogg is a 'swell': a gentleman in good clothes.

00:11:15.187 --> 00:11:18.259
The fare is sixpence a mile — or part of a mile.

00:11:18.539 --> 00:11:23.744
Charing Cross is about a mile and a half away, so call it one shilling: a 'bob'.

00:11:24.024 --> 00:11:28.973
Watch the line on the 1872 map, south and east to the Strand.

00:11:29.253 --> 00:11:34.672
London already had an Underground railway — nine years old, full of steam and smoke.

00:11:34.952 --> 00:11:36.765
Fogg takes a cab instead.

00:11:37.045 --> 00:11:42.954
Listen: hooves on stone, and fast French dance music for Passepartout, the Parisian.

00:11:43.234 --> 00:11:45.773
They left the club at twenty-five past seven.

00:11:46.053 --> 00:11:48.634
They reach Charing Cross at twenty past eight.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.349
This is the Strand, and ahead is Charing Cross station.

00:12:05.629 --> 00:12:08.936
Forget 'old London': for Fogg, this was new London.

00:12:09.216 --> 00:12:14.293
The station opened in January 1864 — eight years old that night.

00:12:14.573 --> 00:12:19.672
The hotel behind the stone cross, and the cross itself: 1865.

00:12:19.952 --> 00:12:22.661
The Underground railway: nine years old.

00:12:22.941 --> 00:12:25.885
The Embankment along the river: two years old.

00:12:26.165 --> 00:12:29.728
And the clock tower we call Big Ben: thirteen years old.

00:12:30.008 --> 00:12:33.848
Much of what Fogg passes tonight is younger than his valet.

00:12:34.128 --> 00:12:40.912
One thing you cannot see: the station's huge iron roof, 155 metres in one span.

00:12:41.192 --> 00:12:44.072
It fell in 1905 and was replaced.

00:12:44.352 --> 00:12:45.845
That is all we will say.

00:12:46.125 --> 00:12:48.429
Then the one warm moment of the evening.

00:12:48.709 --> 00:12:51.546
A barefoot woman with a child asks for money.

00:12:51.826 --> 00:12:55.367
Fogg gives her the twenty guineas he has just won at cards.

00:12:55.647 --> 00:12:57.674
He does not even stop walking.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.208
On the left: a drawing from 1872 — the hotel and forecourt as Fogg saw them.

00:13:22.488 --> 00:13:24.451
On the right: the cross today.

00:13:24.731 --> 00:13:29.360
Six platforms behind it, and still about one train an hour to Dover.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.669
The hotel is still a hotel, under a new name.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.024
Stop here.

00:13:43.304 --> 00:13:44.755
Two things to find.

00:13:45.035 --> 00:13:50.495
The first is straight above you: follow the cross up to its top — twenty-one metres,

00:13:50.495 --> 00:13:51.990
seventy feet, of stone.

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:58.073
It is a copy, put up in 1865 — the year the hotel behind it opened.

00:13:58.353 --> 00:13:59.846
The second is harder.

00:14:00.126 --> 00:14:02.814
Turn to your right, out of the station yard,

00:14:02.814 --> 00:14:06.419
and look about two hundred metres towards Trafalgar Square.

00:14:06.699 --> 00:14:11.462
You cannot quite see him from here, but a statue of King Charles the First rides

00:14:11.462 --> 00:14:11.819
there.

00:14:12.099 --> 00:14:15.640
The first cross, from 1291, stood on that spot.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:19.261
London still measures its distances from that statue,

00:14:19.261 --> 00:14:21.467
not from the stone above your head.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:34.261
Twenty to nine, still under the roof.

00:14:34.541 --> 00:14:36.952
Look up — what is the weather doing tonight?

00:14:37.232 --> 00:14:42.891
A dark night and a fine, steady rain. Verne's own words. Rain on the station roof,

00:14:42.891 --> 00:14:45.168
then rain on the carriage window.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.283
It's twenty to nine, and it's raining.

00:15:02.563 --> 00:15:07.384
That's Passepartout, with the bag — he's known his master for about nine hours.

00:15:07.664 --> 00:15:09.200
Who is your new master?

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.254
<v Passepartout>Mr Phileas Fogg of Savile Row, monsieur

00:15:12.254 --> 00:15:16.594
<v Passepartout>— the most exact and settled gentleman in the United Kingdom,

00:15:16.594 --> 00:15:21.217
<v Passepartout>they told me. Rises at eight, tea and toast at twenty-three past,

00:15:21.217 --> 00:15:27.050
<v Passepartout>out at half past eleven, home at midnight. A real machine. I do not mind serving a

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:31.603
<v Passepartout>machine. I have known him since twenty-nine minutes past eleven.

00:15:32.203 --> 00:15:33.867
We've got a train to catch.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.416
This postcard is from 1905, thirty-three years after Fogg.

00:15:50.696 --> 00:15:56.072
The same platforms — and the old roof, probably in its last months before it fell.

00:15:56.352 --> 00:15:58.528
The boat train to Paris is waiting.

00:15:58.808 --> 00:15:59.981
Now, tonight.

00:16:00.261 --> 00:16:02.394
Twenty to nine: we are seated.

00:16:02.674 --> 00:16:07.154
A quarter to nine: the whistle, and we are off, over Hungerford Bridge.

00:16:07.434 --> 00:16:10.037
Trains still leave Fogg's station this way.

00:16:10.317 --> 00:16:13.474
Our film is daylight; Fogg had rain and dark.

00:16:13.754 --> 00:16:18.959
Look right: the Embankment along the river, two years old in 1872.

00:16:19.239 --> 00:16:21.607
Beyond it, the clock tower, thirteen.

00:16:21.887 --> 00:16:24.234
New London slips away behind him.

00:16:24.514 --> 00:16:27.415
Today this train takes about two hours to Dover.

00:16:27.695 --> 00:16:30.682
Fogg's plan gave him seven days to reach Suez.

00:16:30.962 --> 00:16:32.477
This is the first hour.

00:16:32.757 --> 00:16:35.765
A few miles out, Passepartout sits up straight.

00:16:36.045 --> 00:16:38.797
He has forgotten to turn off the gas in his room.

00:16:39.077 --> 00:16:41.616
'It will burn,' says Fogg, 'at your expense.'

00:16:41.896 --> 00:16:43.219
To be continued.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.491
Your souvenir today: Fogg's last breakfast in London

00:17:11.491 --> 00:17:14.848
— the meal he ate before betting half his fortune.

00:17:15.128 --> 00:17:18.627
The dish is grilled fish with a Reading-sauce-style ketchup.

00:17:18.907 --> 00:17:25.222
Reading sauce was a real English sauce from 1802; it disappeared in the 1960s.

00:17:25.502 --> 00:17:27.315
The recipe is on the card.

00:17:27.595 --> 00:17:31.244
Cook the meal on the evening of the twenty-first of December,

00:17:31.244 --> 00:17:33.696
and sit down to eat by a quarter to nine.

00:17:33.976 --> 00:17:36.237
That is when Fogg promised to be back.

00:17:36.517 --> 00:17:41.317
His motto, and ours: 'A well-used minimum suffices for everything.'
